WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:07.130
Military Forces: We are not just the ones
that sit in the back anymore, we are actually

00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:11.560
leading troops and leading a team, and hopefully
winning a battle out there.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:16.010
13News Now: As we look at the people we need
to attract to serve in our military, if we

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:21.070
don t expand the numbers of women, we are
not going to have the people we need to do

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:22.070
the jobs.

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:29.400
SIERRA: For almost 80 years, women have served
this country in uniform, and today, over 200,000

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.730
serve in active duty forces. They are critical
to the military s success, and our national

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:41.210
security depends on their contributions, sacrifices,
and achievements. But as they protect us,

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:47.250
they are facing a threat that no soldier should
ever have to face.It is estimated that 1 in

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:53.890
4 servicewomen will experience a sexual assault
during their career in the military. And the

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:58.449
military s separate justice system, overseen
by commanders, has been criticized for its

00:00:58.449 --> 00:01:11.380
low rate of conviction. The cost for each
survivor is incalculable. At the same time,

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:17.270
the trend is undermining our military s readiness,
and harming recruitment and retention. Last

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:21.520
week, the Pentagon announced that it will
implement an eight-year plan to curb these

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:28.200
crimes and to begin moving cases outside the
oversight of commanders. But will it be enough?

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:35.220
I m Gabrielle Sierra and this is Why It Matters.
Today, the important role of women in the

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:40.360
military, and the system that has failed them.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:47.810
SIERRA: So what are some of the key facts
that you think people should know when it

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:51.250
comes to sexual assault and the military?

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:58.180
CHRISTENSEN: Well that it is at a crisis number
and it's not getting any better.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:04.020
SIERRA: This is Don Christensen, a retired
Air Force colonel with 23 years of experience

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:10.259
as a military lawyer, also known as a judge
advocate, or a JAG. Currently, he is the President

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:16.010
of Protect Our Defenders, a human rights non-profit
seeking justice for survivors of military

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.670
sexual assault and sexual harassment.

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:24.560
CHRISTENSEN: So the military is required by
Congress to do a survey with active duty force

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.590
every two years. To get an estimate of the
prevalence rate of sexual assault and rape

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:36.700
among the active duty men and women who serve.
And that has been going on since 2006. It

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:41.650
averages well over 20,000 sex assaults a year.
That's just the active duty force, doesn't

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:47.420
count the reserve and guard or civilians that
are sexually assaulted by active duty members.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:52.870
Over six percent of the women are sexually
assaulted or raped in a year, and we see very

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:55.360
little accountability.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:01.620
MYERS: Some people in the Military will say,
" You know, one in four women, in uniform

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:08.810
is sexually assaulted." And that is pretty
on par with the rest of the United States,

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:13.040
the incidents of women being sexually assaulted.
But the big difference is that one in four

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:19.480
American women are not sexually assaulted
at work by their co-workers. And that's the

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:25.010
big rub in the Military, is that these are
your peers. And why is this so prevalent?

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:31.590
SIERRA: This is Meghann Myers, Pentagon Bureau
Chief for Military Times, which provides news

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:35.069
about service members at home and deployed
around the world.

00:03:35.069 --> 00:03:40.120
MYERS: You know, if one in, one in four female
reporters were sexually assaulted by the reporters

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:46.439
in the newsroom, right? Or if one in four
female nurses were sexually assaulted by doctors

00:03:46.439 --> 00:03:52.110
in the hospital, that would be outrageous.
You know, we would never accept that in those,

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:57.980
you know, small small communities, that prevalence.
These are, again, supposed to be the people

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:05.409
that you would die for. And yet, you are,
you know, victimizing them.

00:04:05.409 --> 00:04:11.690
SIERRA: According to the Defense Department
s Annual Report on Sexual Assault in the Military,

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:17.170
a DoD survey indicated that in the fiscal
year of 2018, 20,500 service members were

00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:26.759
sexually assaulted or raped, including 13,000
women and 7,500 men. Of these only 203 incidents

00:04:26.759 --> 00:04:34.509
resulted in conviction. That s less than one
percent. These are wounds that cut deep. A

00:04:34.509 --> 00:04:40.460
2020 DOD report found that sexual assault
in the military is more likely to cause post-traumatic

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:47.740
stress disorder than combat. The statistics
clearly show that both men and women are suffering

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:53.389
sexual assault in the Military, but its a
huge topic, so in this episode we are going

00:04:53.389 --> 00:04:56.419
to be primarily focusing on women.

00:04:56.419 --> 00:05:04.310
SIERRA: So, obviously, the deepest consequence
of this issue exists in the damaged lives

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:11.050
of survivors, the pain that they have had
to endure, but what are the other consequences?

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:14.180
How does this problem hurt the military?

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:20.270
CHRISTENSEN: Well, let's start with this,
in an all-volunteer force, the American Military

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:26.740
cannot meet its requirements if women don't
serve. They make up 15 to 16 percent of the

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:32.569
military. If you take that 15 to 16 percent
out, we do not have enough qualified men who

00:05:32.569 --> 00:05:38.010
are willing to serve to fill that role. Second
thing is just the cost. You know, these women

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:44.919
range from brand new trainees in basic training
to colonels and even generals and admirals

00:05:44.919 --> 00:05:51.570
that are sexually assaulted. They choose overwhelmingly
to leave. That is a huge cost that we have

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:57.759
in that. It costs so much to train a pilot
or train an infantry officer or a medical

00:05:57.759 --> 00:06:03.680
personnel, and to have them choose to leave
or be forced out because of this is a massive

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:06.200
talent pool drain for the services.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:12.520
SIERRA: More than 1 in 4 survivors of sexual
assault, harassment, or discrimination have

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:18.449
taken steps to leave the military. The talent
drain creates serious consequences for military

00:06:18.449 --> 00:06:22.670
capabilities, including for tasks that only
women can perform.

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:30.260
MYERS: So the best example of women in a mission,
in combat, doing something that men could

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:37.700
not do, does involve this most recent operation
in Afghanistan. Basically, the idea was, when

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:43.041
counterinsurgency, which is having troops
go into villages, into neighborhoods, win

00:06:43.041 --> 00:06:46.259
hearts and minds, you know, that was the way,
that was how, what we called it. In order

00:06:46.259 --> 00:06:51.000
to integrate with those people and to learn
about what they needed in the village, getting

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:56.620
women on board, being able to talk to women
about what they needed was crucial. And in

00:06:56.620 --> 00:07:02.009
these very conservative Muslim communities,
women are not allowed to talk to men who are

00:07:02.009 --> 00:07:06.009
not in their families. So they could not talk
to the male soldiers, who were coming to the

00:07:06.009 --> 00:07:10.960
village to speak to the village elders. And
so special operations decided, "Okay, how

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:16.680
about we train, you know, some teams of women
who can go in and do that engagement?" And

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:19.949
that is what they've been doing all along.
A woman, Ashley White was her name, actually

00:07:19.949 --> 00:07:24.090
died in combat in Afghanistan in one of these
teams when she was serving with Army Special

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:29.870
Forces. So while women who serve just want
to be seen as a service member like any other

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:36.300
service member, there are examples like that
where being a woman made somebody more useful

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:40.069
in a situation, than the men who they were
serving with.

00:07:40.069 --> 00:07:45.410
SIERRA: So how does sexual assault impact
units on the ground?

00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:51.139
MYERS: So you could ask anybody in the military,
you know, "What are the most important things

00:07:51.139 --> 00:07:58.900
for combat effectiveness?" And unit cohesion
is chief among them. Which means everybody

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:04.840
is watching out for each other, they're on
the same page, they're willing to, you know,

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:10.199
defend each other under any circumstance.
And along those lines, there are also senior

00:08:10.199 --> 00:08:15.110
leaders in the military who will referred
to sexual assault as fratricide, which is

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:19.510
basically killing your brother or sister,
attacking your brother or sister. And that

00:08:19.510 --> 00:08:24.520
is, you know, ideally the way that senior
leaders want the issue to be looked at, because

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:33.460
when women in a unit do not feel safe, you
have destroyed the credibility, and the combat

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:38.360
effectiveness of that unit because you are
supposed to be watching out for each other,

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:42.529
and obviously, if you're assaulting each other,
it's not watching for each other.

00:08:42.529 --> 00:08:47.360
SIERRA: To truly understand what s going on
here, it s important to understand the long

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:52.850
historical struggle for equality in the military,
and the way women s service roles have changed

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:54.140
over time.

00:08:54.140 --> 00:09:00.860
MYERS: When women were first formally able
to serve in uniform dates back to World War

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:07.200
II, there were a couple of voluntary organizations
within the Army and the Navy, and those were

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:14.540
largely medical, healthcare, and otherwise,
you know, secretarial administrative roles.

00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:20.060
Women started to be more involved in direct
action, you know, if you will, more into the

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:23.730
eighties and the nineties women were when
women were first allowed to serve on ships,

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:29.480
when women were allowed to pilot aircraft.
And then things were very status quo for a

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:33.290
while. In the early nineties, there was a
ban on women in direct combat roles. So that

00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:41.050
was, you know, no infantry, no artillery,
no tanks, things like that. The feelings about

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:46.060
that really started to change because of the
global war on terror, because when we were

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:50.880
in Iraq and Afghanistan, it really didn't
matter if you were, you know, a female mechanic,

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:56.750
a female military police officer, you know,
a female supply sergeant, women were getting

00:09:56.750 --> 00:10:03.420
shot at and getting blown up just as often,
you know, as men were, so women were in combat

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:07.260
despite not being allowed to really serve
in roles that were traditionally seen that

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:13.010
way. In 2016, Ash Carter, who was the Secretary
of Defense then, he decided, I'd like to get

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:17.200
rid of the combat exemption for women, period,
you know, we want women to be able to serve

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:21.630
in all roles unless the services can come
up with a good reason why not, and the services

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:28.529
elected not to make any excuses. And so all
of those roles did end up opening around 2017,

00:10:28.529 --> 00:10:33.480
2018 is when you started seeing more and more
women assigned, particularly in Army and Marine

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:38.760
Corps infantry, which were kind of the last
bastions of male-only environments in the

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:43.141
military. And then more and more into special
operations roles as well. You know, there

00:10:43.141 --> 00:10:49.330
are now women who serve in army special forces.
There are women in air force special operations,

00:10:49.330 --> 00:10:52.010
and that gap is closing more than ever.

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:58.029
SIERRA: Today, women in the military are just
as essential as their male counterparts. But

00:10:58.029 --> 00:11:03.360
they have only had equal opportunities for
a handful of years. As a result, many vestiges

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:08.260
of the military s traditionally all-male culture
remain.

00:11:08.260 --> 00:11:16.149
MYERS: I think a lot of it has to do with
the way that women were integrated into the

00:11:16.149 --> 00:11:22.890
military, which was incredibly traumatic and
problematic for a long time. They were degraded,

00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:29.040
made to feel other, less than the entire time.
And that dehumanization, and that degradation

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:34.950
of women and their contributions really made
it a lot easier for their peers to harass

00:11:34.950 --> 00:11:42.250
and assault them. And that sort of, othering
of women in the military still continues,

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:48.630
up and to the point where the Marine Corps
is just now integrating boot camp. The Marine

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:55.520
Corps has never let men and women train together
from the very first days in the service. And

00:11:55.520 --> 00:12:00.710
that created a culture where there were Marines,
and then there were female Marines. And a

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:04.790
lot of that came from the fact that, you know,
"She doesn't do the same training as I did,

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:10.260
she's less than me. We have different fitness
standards. And that leads up to this dehumanization

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:15.790
that then makes people feel empowered to victimize
women that they serve with. So that's the

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:20.310
other part that s gonna take a much longer
time for them to tackle, versus you know,

00:12:20.310 --> 00:12:22.630
changing the way that they prosecute these
things.

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:28.370
SIERRA: That said, the way these crimes are
prosecuted is an important part of the picture.

00:12:28.370 --> 00:12:33.201
Crimes committed in the military are tried
in a completely separate justice system. This

00:12:33.201 --> 00:12:38.680
system is overseen by commanders, rather than
civilian judges, and it operates according

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:41.220
to very different rules.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:45.760
SIERRA:
So, other than what I ve learned in movies

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:51.050
like A Few Good Men, I don t know much about
the military justice system. Do you think

00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:55.019
you walk me through how a sexual assault case
might be handled?

00:12:55.019 --> 00:13:01.910
MYERS: So the first step following an assault
is a report, and you can report that to a

00:13:01.910 --> 00:13:07.730
commander, to anyone in leadership, really.
And the idea is that it will get routed to

00:13:07.730 --> 00:13:13.091
a criminal investigation unit. If you decide
that you would like it to be prosecuted. There

00:13:13.091 --> 00:13:17.660
are two kinds of reports in the military:
restricted reports, which are basically for

00:13:17.660 --> 00:13:22.410
data collection purposes, but don't result
in an investigation, and then unrestricted

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:26.860
ones that turn into an investigation. And
so you will see one of the services that are

00:13:26.860 --> 00:13:31.810
local investigation units will come by do
a criminal investigation, that's you, you

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:37.831
know, you might be familiar with in the civilian
world, but the big difference in the military

00:13:37.831 --> 00:13:44.240
is rather than that case being referred to
a prosecutor in a district attorney's office,

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:49.980
the commander of that unit gets to decide
whether charges are pressed. And then the

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:55.520
commander of that unit gets to decide whether
it goes to trial. So it is not at this point,

00:13:55.520 --> 00:14:01.509
an independently adjudicated system, a judge,
and a jury do preside over the trial. But

00:14:01.509 --> 00:14:07.300
again, the commander has the authority to
overturn the verdict or the sentencing in

00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:11.320
that trial, if he or she feels that it was
not warranted.

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:16.670
SIERRA: That judge and jury, by the way, are
also military personnel.

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:24.010
MYERS: So that's everything from mishandling
weapons, from, you know, vehicle accidents,

00:14:24.010 --> 00:14:28.800
DUIs, anything that happens, the commanders
administer justice in the military, as it

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:30.060
is now.

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:36.079
SIERRA: What are the obstacles a survivor
may face when thinking about coming forward?

00:14:36.079 --> 00:14:40.560
CHRISTENSEN: Unlike the civilian system, where
if someone is sexually assaulted and they

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:45.089
choose to report, they're gonna go to law
enforcement. In the military, it's controlled

00:14:45.089 --> 00:14:52.040
by the commander, the person who's the boss
of the suspect and often the boss of the victim.

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:55.020
They're gonna have a divided loyalty in that
case -

00:14:55.020 --> 00:14:56.020
SIERRA: Right.

00:14:56.020 --> 00:15:00.290
CHRISTENSEN: Which one do they choose? And
everything is revolved there. The investigation

00:15:00.290 --> 00:15:06.920
occurs there, the prosecution, if it happens,
it occurs there. A victim's commander reviews

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:14.050
all the evidence. That victim's private life
is being ripped open. If there has been a

00:15:14.050 --> 00:15:18.940
rape kit, the commander gets to see the results
of that. If there were photographs taken of

00:15:18.940 --> 00:15:23.430
the rape kit, commander gets to see that.
If intimate images were taken, which often

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:29.459
happens, the commander gets to see that. That's
going to disincentivize a lot of people, knowing

00:15:29.459 --> 00:15:35.850
that day in and day out, this guy that you
work for has been able to look at the most

00:15:35.850 --> 00:15:39.250
horrible part of your life, and you're gonna
be thinking about that.

00:15:39.250 --> 00:15:44.600
SIERRA: Right. I mean, you're reliving something
that's just so horrible over again, but then

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:49.000
also very publicly with, essentially, your
colleagues.

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:50.000
CHRISTENSEN: Yeah.

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:55.269
CHRISTENSEN: So the DOD also has to look at
retaliation thanks to Congress, and we know

00:15:55.269 --> 00:16:00.880
from those same surveys that of the women
and men who come forward, about 64 percent

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:04.230
of them say they were retaliated against after
they -

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:05.230
SIERRA: Wow.

00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:09.819
CHRISTENSEN: Came forward. Yeah, it's huge
numbers. The DODIG released a report in 2016

00:16:09.819 --> 00:16:15.290
showing that one out of three women who reported
the sexual assault, were forced out of the

00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:20.940
military within a year of reporting. Over
20 percent of them had a less than fully honorable

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:26.852
discharge. Retaliation looks like career consequences,
where you're not allowed to progress or you

00:16:26.852 --> 00:16:31.940
don't get promoted. But retaliation is also
social retaliation and that can be incredibly

00:16:31.940 --> 00:16:37.740
painful, because in the military environment,
your entire life revolves around that installation.

00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:42.870
You eat there, you sleep there, you work there,
you shop there, you go to the movies there.

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:43.870
Everything is right there.

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:49.709
SIERRA: When we learn about the justice system
in civics class, a lot of time is spent focusing

00:16:49.709 --> 00:16:55.389
on impartiality. That s a core tenant of how
justice is supposed to be carried out in the

00:16:55.389 --> 00:17:01.120
U.S. But critics of the military system point
out that commanders often face a conflict

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:03.570
of interest when deciding cases.

00:17:03.570 --> 00:17:10.190
MYERS: So, the commander is accountable for
the good order and discipline of the unit,

00:17:10.190 --> 00:17:16.689
that also means that a commander can face
their own repercussions for not having control

00:17:16.689 --> 00:17:23.569
over the good order and discipline of a unit.
And so, there's also a very high temptation

00:17:23.569 --> 00:17:31.660
to under report, push things aside, couch
incidents, so that the commander doesn't look

00:17:31.660 --> 00:17:38.160
bad. And that becomes a real conflict of interest,
obviously, when you have a rape in your formation,

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:42.590
and you have to decide whether there's gonna
be a trial or not because your name is gonna

00:17:42.590 --> 00:17:44.010
be on that.

00:17:44.010 --> 00:17:49.790
SIERRA: Do victims have the option of just
opting out of all of this, and going to the

00:17:49.790 --> 00:17:51.490
civilian police?

00:17:51.490 --> 00:17:58.791
MYERS: It depends. If it happens on a military
base, no. On a military base, the military

00:17:58.791 --> 00:18:03.679
is responsible for justice. If it's something
that happens, you know, at an off base residence,

00:18:03.679 --> 00:18:09.020
or an off base business, something like that
especially if it's service member on service

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:17.650
member, generally the law enforcement in military
communities will defer to commanders on the

00:18:17.650 --> 00:18:20.120
base, for adjudicating something like that.

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:25.670
SIERRA: The military justice system comes
from a very old tradition, one that was aimed

00:18:25.670 --> 00:18:30.350
at maintaining order within the ranks and
the structure of command. Advocates of this

00:18:30.350 --> 00:18:35.010
system say that taking cases out of the chain
of command would undermine the authority of

00:18:35.010 --> 00:18:40.070
commanders and therefore compromise discipline
and battle effectiveness. But there are some

00:18:40.070 --> 00:18:43.880
examples from other countries that seem to
contradict that prediction.

00:18:43.880 --> 00:18:49.200
SIERRA: Is this unique to the United States?
I mean, do other major countries have similar

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:51.440
legal rules for their military?

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:57.230
CHRISTENSEN: Most of our allies have moved
to a case where the military commander does

00:18:57.230 --> 00:19:04.210
not make prosecution decisions, the default
is to go to the civilian prosecutors.

00:19:04.210 --> 00:19:10.380
SIERRA: Australia, Germany, Canada, Great
Britain and Israel prosecute major crimes

00:19:10.380 --> 00:19:15.770
outside the chain of command by professional
military prosecutors. And by most reasonable

00:19:15.770 --> 00:19:18.650
measures, their militaries have remained effective.

00:19:18.650 --> 00:19:24.780
Israel has come down particularly hard on
this issue, with IDF Chief of Staff, Aviv

00:19:24.780 --> 00:19:31.059
Kohavi, saying Any attack on sexual grounds,
in speech or in deed, is an attack on human

00:19:31.059 --> 00:19:40.210
dignity and constitutes a severe breach. As
we look for answers at home or abroad, and

00:19:40.210 --> 00:19:46.400
comb through so many complexities, we risk
forgetting the living, breathing human beings

00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:51.640
who have suffered as a result of this problem.
So we decided to speak to a survivor to hear

00:19:51.640 --> 00:19:56.789
her story. She served with the National Guard,
which essentially follows the same justice

00:19:56.789 --> 00:20:00.860
protocols as other active duty branches.

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:08.020
SEXTON: My name is Heather Sexton and I'm
currently a biologist at a pharmaceutical

00:20:08.020 --> 00:20:15.570
testing company, but I used to be a Captain
in the Missouri Army National Guard.

00:20:15.570 --> 00:20:19.730
SIERRA: Can tell us a little bit about your
assault.

00:20:19.730 --> 00:20:29.460
SEXTON: Yeah. We were going to a huge event
in Utah, and I was part of the Advon team,

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:33.940
which basically means there's a team that
gets sent ahead of time to set everything

00:20:33.940 --> 00:20:38.230
up so that when the exercise starts, it's
all ready to go and everyone else just has

00:20:38.230 --> 00:20:43.990
to show up and start training. So it was a
small group of us, probably about eight people.

00:20:43.990 --> 00:20:53.480
And I was the highest-ranking, so I was in
charge of everyone. And I was also the only

00:20:53.480 --> 00:21:00.180
female there. There was this really long,
dark sidewalk leading down to the female barracks,

00:21:00.180 --> 00:21:06.770
and just because there's supposed to be, like,
30 different states there, and it was just

00:21:06.770 --> 00:21:11.800
this huge conglomeration of people, I wasn't
really comfortable walking back to the barracks

00:21:11.800 --> 00:21:16.900
after we were done setting up late at night.
So I started having some of the members of

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:22.080
my team walk me back to my barracks, which
is honestly a little embarrassing when you're

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:27.890
the highest-ranking one there, but I felt
like it was really necessary at that point.

00:21:27.890 --> 00:21:33.760
SIERRA: Heather told us that on one of these
evenings at the end of the walk she was sexually

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:35.470
assaulted by a team member.

00:21:35.470 --> 00:21:41.679
SEXTON: The next morning I went and reported
everything to the Chaplain and the victim

00:21:41.679 --> 00:21:48.029
advocate there, but that was in Utah, so it
doesn't really correlate to our state since

00:21:48.029 --> 00:21:52.830
I was in the National Guard. So basically
they got me out of there, but I kind of had

00:21:52.830 --> 00:21:59.550
to redo everything. All of the reporting once
I got back to Missouri.

00:21:59.550 --> 00:22:04.610
SIERRA: What was the atmosphere like when
you went to report the assault that second

00:22:04.610 --> 00:22:05.610
time?

00:22:05.610 --> 00:22:12.929
SEXTON: It was honestly awful. Immediately
everybody knew. But the worst part was that

00:22:12.929 --> 00:22:19.140
they had immediately transferred him to a
new unit and brought him home from Utah, and

00:22:19.140 --> 00:22:25.340
the new unit they transferred him to was the
unit I had to go to report it. So I was standing

00:22:25.340 --> 00:22:30.900
there two days after, and he walked in the
office.

00:22:30.900 --> 00:22:39.980
SIERRA: Oh my gosh. What was the process for
handling your case? You know, were you optimistic

00:22:39.980 --> 00:22:43.620
about them doing the right thing as it went
along?

00:22:43.620 --> 00:22:51.240
SEXTON: No, not really. I had heard a lot
of horror stories. That's why I wasn't sure

00:22:51.240 --> 00:22:56.800
initially if I wanted to report and go through
it or not, but then in the end I know I would

00:22:56.800 --> 00:23:03.350
have, because that would have haunted me forever.
Even now knowing the outcome, I don't regret

00:23:03.350 --> 00:23:05.000
reporting it.

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:12.799
SIERRA: So, how did the assault change your
experience at work? Because, you know, you

00:23:12.799 --> 00:23:17.630
waited for the system to do its work. How
long did you wait, and what was it, you know,

00:23:17.630 --> 00:23:22.179
like for you continuing to report and continuing
to work through that?

00:23:22.179 --> 00:23:24.890
SEXTON: It was almost two years.

00:23:24.890 --> 00:23:25.890
SIERRA: Wow.

00:23:25.890 --> 00:23:36.159
SEXTON: And it was terrible. That was a small
unit, it was embarrassing. He was friends

00:23:36.159 --> 00:23:43.230
with everybody. It was tough going and showing
up knowing that everyone knew and knowing

00:23:43.230 --> 00:23:45.330
everyone didn't have my back.

00:23:45.330 --> 00:23:51.490
SIERRA: Yeah. So, you know, what was the ultimate
outcome?

00:23:51.490 --> 00:23:59.490
SEXTON: They validated my whole story, they
said that everything happened, but ultimately

00:23:59.490 --> 00:24:08.340
my claim was unsubstantiated. I honestly was
so confused. I thought I had mentally prepared

00:24:08.340 --> 00:24:14.140
myself for everything, I thought I already
knew how it works, but I never thought they

00:24:14.140 --> 00:24:21.700
would tell me it happened, but it's okay that
it happened. So they just treated it like

00:24:21.700 --> 00:24:28.020
a regular business meeting at drills, said,
"Can you come in the office really quick?"

00:24:28.020 --> 00:24:32.929
And just read it off to me, like it was, I
don't know, I couldn't believe it. It had

00:24:32.929 --> 00:24:40.940
been destroying my life for two years and
they just acted like it was a regular meeting.

00:24:40.940 --> 00:24:49.630
And then on top of that, to really rub salt
in the wound, they gave me a letter from the

00:24:49.630 --> 00:24:55.600
Brigadier General, the second-highest-ranking
officer in the state of Missouri, reprimanding

00:24:55.600 --> 00:25:00.000
me, because I should have been more professional
that night.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:02.880
SIERRA: So how long did you stay after that?

00:25:02.880 --> 00:25:10.760
SEXTON: I got out as soon as I could, but
that wasn't very quickly. So I got the verdict

00:25:10.760 --> 00:25:15.649
in January, I believe, and I was out by April.

00:25:15.649 --> 00:25:18.160
SIERRA: I am just so incredibly sorry.

00:25:18.160 --> 00:25:24.220
SEXTON: Thank you, I really appreciate you
guys shining light on it too. It's obviously

00:25:24.220 --> 00:25:27.070
really important to me.

00:25:27.070 --> 00:25:31.730
SIERRA: We reached out to the Missouri Army
National Guard and they replied that they

00:25:31.730 --> 00:25:37.620
were prohibited from commenting on personnel
matters or investigations. Heather s story

00:25:37.620 --> 00:25:43.740
is just one of many thousands. And, according
to the statistics, in recent years less than

00:25:43.740 --> 00:25:51.320
1% of survivors have seen their case carried
through to conviction.

00:25:51.320 --> 00:25:58.980
SIERRA: So what sort of sentencing do we see
in the Military system? You know, what are

00:25:58.980 --> 00:26:02.020
the punishments? How often are they handed
out?

00:26:02.020 --> 00:26:08.039
MYERS: It can be all over the place. You know,
anywhere from, a discharge, something other

00:26:08.039 --> 00:26:14.320
than an honorable discharge. And you know,
docking pay, or, reducing someone in rank.

00:26:14.320 --> 00:26:19.270
You know, there are people who are in prison
for rape in the Military. It's very rare that

00:26:19.270 --> 00:26:26.289
that happens, only because, much like in the
civilian world, out of thousands of reports

00:26:26.289 --> 00:26:31.450
that they get every year, a small handful
of those will go to trial and an even smaller

00:26:31.450 --> 00:26:35.320
handful will result in a conviction, and a
sentence.

00:26:35.320 --> 00:26:40.510
SIERRA: So what has the military done to address
this over the years?

00:26:40.510 --> 00:26:46.140
CHRISTENSEN: One of the things they have done
is, basically try to train their way out of

00:26:46.140 --> 00:26:52.860
this crisis. That hasn't been effective. The
training, I think, falls on, deaf ears. It

00:26:52.860 --> 00:26:58.690
has resulted, I think, a lot of backlash.
There's, like, a term, being SHARPed. SHARP

00:26:58.690 --> 00:27:06.060
stands for Sexual Harassment Assault Response
Program. That's an Army program. And so they'll

00:27:06.060 --> 00:27:12.130
use that as a derogatory term to a soldier's
complaint. "Oh, she's trying to SHARP somebody.

00:27:12.130 --> 00:27:14.039
Like, that's made up.

00:27:14.039 --> 00:27:23.010
MYERS: The feeling here among senior leadership
is basically that the awareness and the infrastructure

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:28.500
that the services have built around trying
to, first trying to respond to it, but also

00:27:28.500 --> 00:27:35.049
prevent it, that has been going on for nearly
a decade now. And there have been no measurable

00:27:35.049 --> 00:27:41.570
drops in the incidents of sexual assaults
and no measurable increases in the prosecutions

00:27:41.570 --> 00:27:47.890
or convictions of sexual assaults. And so
the feeling is, we tried all these other things,

00:27:47.890 --> 00:27:54.169
a lot of very smart, experienced people think
that changing the way we prosecute them may

00:27:54.169 --> 00:28:00.650
make a difference. And so they are willing
to try that to see if the numbers bear out.

00:28:00.650 --> 00:28:07.800
SIERRA: For decades, efforts focused on prevention,
victim support, and bystander intervention.

00:28:07.800 --> 00:28:13.470
The educational content was disseminated through
videos, posters, and classes. But experts

00:28:13.470 --> 00:28:18.980
say this strategy missed the mark. And as
time went on, repeated attempts to fix this

00:28:18.980 --> 00:28:24.559
problem failed. But things may be reaching
a turning point. Last week, the Department

00:28:24.559 --> 00:28:30.520
of Defense announced a plan that aims to comprehensively
address this issue. Chief among its proposals

00:28:30.520 --> 00:28:35.240
is a change that would move prosecutorial
decisions outside the chain of command in

00:28:35.240 --> 00:28:39.990
cases of sexual assault. The news broke after
our guest interviews, but we reached back

00:28:39.990 --> 00:28:46.220
out to them for comment. Both agreed that
the plan contains many good ideas. However,

00:28:46.220 --> 00:28:51.990
they said that its 8 year implementation timeline
could be reversed by a new Administration

00:28:51.990 --> 00:28:58.059
or a new Secretary of Defense. And despite
this major push, the DoD still cannot remove

00:28:58.059 --> 00:29:03.039
authority from the chain of command without
congress. The Department of Defense is not

00:29:03.039 --> 00:29:08.110
the only potential source of reform. Before
the announcement, there had already been two

00:29:08.110 --> 00:29:13.179
proposals in Congress to address the problem.
In the Senate there is the Military Justice

00:29:13.179 --> 00:29:17.720
Improvement and Increasing Prevention Act.
Helmed by Kirsten Gillibrand, it has garnered

00:29:17.720 --> 00:29:20.289
bipartisan support and is expected to pass.

00:29:20.289 --> 00:29:23.850
Kristien GILLIBRAND, PBS News: There s few
bills in Congress that have the support of

00:29:23.850 --> 00:29:30.149
Bernie Sanders and Ted Cruz, both Chuck Schumer
and Mitch McConnell, but this bill does.

00:29:30.149 --> 00:29:34.730
SIERRA: And in the House, there is a similar
bill, the Vanessa Guillen Military Justice

00:29:34.730 --> 00:29:37.130
Improvement and Increasing Prevention Act.

00:29:37.130 --> 00:29:42.269
There are still some questions about how the
Congressional legislation and the DoD s plan

00:29:42.269 --> 00:29:48.690
will interact. But, if passed, they would
be some of the most significant military reforms

00:29:48.690 --> 00:29:50.000
of our time.

00:29:50.000 --> 00:29:56.380
MYERS: The Pentagon announced earlier this
year that they will start taking steps toward

00:29:56.380 --> 00:30:01.271
removing sexual assaults and what they consider
related crimes, so like I said earlier, sexual

00:30:01.271 --> 00:30:07.650
harassment, domestic violence, child abuse,
out of the chain of command. But kind of in

00:30:07.650 --> 00:30:14.240
a, in a parallel lane, you have, legislation
from New York Senator, Kirsten Gillibrand,

00:30:14.240 --> 00:30:18.620
that has a lot of support in the Senate, and
likewise, a decent amount of support in the

00:30:18.620 --> 00:30:24.669
House of Representatives that would take all
major crimes out of the chain of command,

00:30:24.669 --> 00:30:28.429
away from a commander's authority. It's a
little fuzzy as to where the definition is,

00:30:28.429 --> 00:30:35.070
but that could be all felonies. So you know,
that could be assaults, and DUIs, and things

00:30:35.070 --> 00:30:42.039
that are crimes, but are not inherently related
to military service. It is possible that those

00:30:42.039 --> 00:30:47.510
things will all be handled independently,
still within the Military, but not at a commander's

00:30:47.510 --> 00:30:53.299
discretion. So a commander's discretion would
go more toward, you know, accidents on a shooting

00:30:53.299 --> 00:31:00.250
range or, you know, vehicle accidents, mishandling,
and that sort of thing.

00:31:00.250 --> 00:31:04.480
SIERRA: Where does the legislation currently
stand?

00:31:04.480 --> 00:31:10.960
MYERS: It was part of the, the most recent
National Defense Authorization Act, which

00:31:10.960 --> 00:31:15.010
is the law that Congress passes every year
that basically tells the Military how it's

00:31:15.010 --> 00:31:20.260
going to spend its money. But that is all
kind of in a holding pattern right now because

00:31:20.260 --> 00:31:24.559
it's such an immense bill. And so, the House
and the Senate, like they always do, have

00:31:24.559 --> 00:31:29.710
gone back and forth about which version they're
most interested in. So it will be a little

00:31:29.710 --> 00:31:35.450
while longer, until we know whether that ends
up in that law. And also, you know, there's,

00:31:35.450 --> 00:31:41.299
there's negotiations going on about whether
Congress even needs to get involved now because

00:31:41.299 --> 00:31:45.040
the Pentagon said, "Okay we're gonna do sexual
assaults, you know. We're gonna fix this,

00:31:45.040 --> 00:31:49.149
this was the most important thing." And so
maybe they will back off on, on taking all

00:31:49.149 --> 00:31:51.519
other felonies out of the chain.

00:31:51.519 --> 00:31:55.409
SIERRA: So what's your take on these different
proposals?

00:31:55.409 --> 00:32:00.889
CHRISTENSEN: Well, I firmly believe that Senator
Gillibrand and Representative Speier have

00:32:00.889 --> 00:32:06.160
the right view on this, that we should be
moving all felonies over. Senator Gillibrand,

00:32:06.160 --> 00:32:12.860
Senator Ernst, have over 60 cosponsors in
the Senate, they have the majority of the

00:32:12.860 --> 00:32:19.940
Senate Armed Services Committee, her legislation
was put into the markup of the National Defense

00:32:19.940 --> 00:32:26.570
Authorization Act. So it looks like it's going
to come out of the Senate version. So I think

00:32:26.570 --> 00:32:32.460
as long as they're part of the NDAA, and they're
virtually identical, which they are, it's

00:32:32.460 --> 00:32:34.830
a really good chance they'll pass.

00:32:34.830 --> 00:32:40.690
SIERRA: The future of the legislation seems
bright. And that s a good thing, because if

00:32:40.690 --> 00:32:46.660
a solution is not found, the problem could
hinder recruitment efforts for years to come.

00:32:46.660 --> 00:32:53.630
SIERRA: So what's lost, if the U.S. military
can't retain and promote women?

00:32:53.630 --> 00:33:03.059
MYERS: The Military will become, as some people
already argue it is, out of touch with American

00:33:03.059 --> 00:33:08.850
people, with American civilization, with what
goes on in the outside world, and that goal

00:33:08.850 --> 00:33:15.420
that they have to reflect the people that
they serve will be lost. And, there are people

00:33:15.420 --> 00:33:20.020
who argue that, back in the '80s and '90s,
when women were all in support roles and were

00:33:20.020 --> 00:33:25.779
pretty marginalized, that you know, we were
still the greatest fighting force. But the

00:33:25.779 --> 00:33:31.669
issue becomes, you know, we're living in this
modern world, this modern world includes women

00:33:31.669 --> 00:33:36.410
in, you know, leadership roles, and it only
stands to reason that you would see that in

00:33:36.410 --> 00:33:41.500
the military as well. And the other thing
is that the services are having harder and

00:33:41.500 --> 00:33:49.620
harder time attracting high quality recruits
to man these organizations. And so, if you

00:33:49.620 --> 00:33:56.610
alienate women from that population you are
alienating roughly 50% of the American people.

00:33:56.610 --> 00:34:05.809
And kind of hamstringing your chances of having
a fully manned force.

00:34:05.809 --> 00:34:17.349
SIERRA: Is there anything that, you know,
you feel like you wanna add? Anything that

00:34:17.349 --> 00:34:18.349
you feel like the story is missing?

00:34:18.349 --> 00:34:26.109
SEXTON: No, I think the only thing I'll say
is that I just really appreciate all the people

00:34:26.109 --> 00:34:31.740
that are still pushing and still trying to
make a change. And don't give up. It seems

00:34:31.740 --> 00:34:55.659
like we're getting close.
